1
00:00:05,180 --> 00:00:09,640
弗里曼：地球——我们都知道的地方
Earth -- a place we all know.

2
00:00:11,500 --> 00:00:16,230
但这是一个恐龙统治人类的地球
But this is an Earth where dinosaurs terrorize mankind.

3
00:00:19,540 --> 00:00:22,030
或者可能是另一个
Or it could be another Earth

4
00:00:22,140 --> 00:00:24,730
苏联赢得登月竞赛的地球
where Russia won the moon race.

5
00:00:24,840 --> 00:00:31,200
或者一眨眼的时间能自我毁灭的地球
Or another Earth that could annihilate our own in the blink of an eye.

6
00:00:31,320 --> 00:00:34,410
在昏暗的星体之后
Beyond the faintest star,

7
00:00:34,520 --> 00:00:37,480
在黑洞的末端
at the bottom of a black hole,

8
00:00:37,590 --> 00:00:39,980
或者藏在更高的维度之中
or hidden in higher dimensions,

9
00:00:40,090 --> 00:00:42,180
突破性的科学表明
groundbreaking science now suggests

10
00:00:42,290 --> 00:00:47,090
事实的版本可能不止一个
there might be more than one version of reality.

11
00:00:47,200 --> 00:00:50,430
存在平行宇宙吗？
Are there parallel universes?

12
00:00:51,010 --> 00:00:53,210
科学频道 - 穿越虫洞 2x06
Science.Channel - Through the Wormhole 2x06

13
00:00:53,530 --> 00:00:57,860
空间 时间 生命本身
Space,time,life itself.

14
00:01:00,580 --> 00:01:04,840
宇宙的秘密，尽在穿越虫洞
The secrets of the cosmos lie Through the Wormhole.

15
00:01:10,300 --> 00:01:15,700
存在平行宇宙吗？
Are there parallel universes?

16
00:01:18,660 --> 00:01:21,620
有不止一个你吗？
ls there more than one of you?

17
00:01:21,730 --> 00:01:23,890
有不止一个我吗？
More than one of me?

18
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:28,460
另一个我看起来可能和我完全相同
Another version of me may look identical.

19
00:01:28,570 --> 00:01:30,330
但我们并不同
But we're not.

20
00:01:30,440 --> 00:01:35,030
我们的命运可能完全不同
Our fates could be completely different.

21
00:01:35,150 --> 00:01:39,170
我们中的一个可能是另一个的派生物
One of us could be a descendent of the other.

22
00:01:39,290 --> 00:01:43,280
我们相遇可能使我们俩都
Our meeting could cause both of us

23
00:01:43,390 --> 00:01:47,010
蒸发成纯能量
to evaporate into pure energy.

24
00:01:47,130 --> 00:01:50,920
我们都可以过着多重的平行的生活方式
We could all be living multiple parallel lives,

25
00:01:51,030 --> 00:01:53,290
因为随着科学家探索
because as scientists explore

26
00:01:53,400 --> 00:01:56,990
外太空的物理和宇宙
the outer reaches of physics and the cosmos,

27
00:01:57,100 --> 00:01:59,260
他们开始相信
they're beginning to believe

28
00:01:59,370 --> 00:02:02,130
平行宇宙确实存在
that parallel universes do exist

29
00:02:02,240 --> 00:02:05,840
并且可能决定人类的命运
and that they might determine the fate of humanity.

30
00:02:09,050 --> 00:02:13,910
当我上小学时，被这个女孩迷住了
When l was in grade school, l was smitten with this girl.

31
00:02:14,020 --> 00:02:17,080
我不能自拔
l couldn't get her out of my mind.

32
00:02:17,190 --> 00:02:20,820
麻烦的是，我有一个滑稽的表达方式
Trouble was, l had a funny way of showing it.

33
00:02:22,560 --> 00:02:25,120
摩根
Morgan!

34
00:02:25,230 --> 00:02:29,090
我常常希望我能成为
l often wished l could be someone else,

35
00:02:29,200 --> 00:02:33,390
有信心和魅力的某个人
somebody with confidence and charm.

36
00:02:33,510 --> 00:02:37,960
我希望自己能换成那个摩根
l wished l could swap myself out for that Morgan,

37
00:02:38,080 --> 00:02:41,170
无论他在哪
wherever he was.

38
00:02:41,280 --> 00:02:45,700
宇宙学家Max Tegmark
Cosmologist Max Tegmark of M.l.T.

39
00:02:45,820 --> 00:02:48,780
相信 其他我
believes that this other version of me,

40
00:02:48,890 --> 00:02:51,440
以及其他我们真地存在
and of all of us, really exists.

41
00:02:51,560 --> 00:02:54,150
并且他相信我们的宇宙的另一个我
And he believes our cosmic doppelgangers

42
00:02:54,260 --> 00:02:57,580
生活在我们已知宇宙的边缘
live beyond the edge of the known universe.

43
00:02:57,990 --> 00:03:01,890
Tegmark：我们现有的关于宇宙构成的最好理论
Tegmark:The best theory we have for what made our Universe

44
00:03:01,960 --> 00:03:04,900
表明它不仅仅是非常，非常地大
predicts that it isn't just really, really big

45
00:03:04,910 --> 00:03:05,590
而且简直是无穷尽地
but actually infinite,

46
00:03:05,600 --> 00:03:09,230
向各个方向延伸
going on literally forever in all directions.

47
00:03:09,940 --> 00:03:13,600
弗里曼：对于Max来说，它完全是统计学上的一个麻烦
For Max, it's all a matter of statistics.

48
00:03:13,710 --> 00:03:16,040
并且在无限的宇宙中
And in an infinite universe,

49
00:03:16,150 --> 00:03:20,550
甚至极不可能的事情都成为可能
even extremely unlikely occurrences are possible.

50
00:03:22,990 --> 00:03:25,180
Tegmark：如果我在这取3块石头
lf l take three rocks here,

51
00:03:25,290 --> 00:03:27,950
橙色的，白色的以及黑色的
here's a pattern of orange, white, and black.

52
00:03:28,060 --> 00:03:29,480
我看着周围
l look around.

53
00:03:29,600 --> 00:03:32,290
这是另外一块——橙色的，白色的及黑色的
Oh, here's another one -- orange, white, and black.

54
00:03:32,400 --> 00:03:35,560
如果取更大范围的10块石头
lf l take a larger pattern with, say, uh, 10 rocks here,

55
00:03:35,670 --> 00:03:39,790
那么我就不得不努力看着寻找它们的副本
then l have to look a lot harder to find a copy of it.

56
00:03:39,910 --> 00:03:42,060
在这它们重复着
Here it repeats.

57
00:03:42,170 --> 00:03:46,170
弗里曼：地球是一个巨大的石堆
Earth is an enormous pile of stones,

58
00:03:46,280 --> 00:03:50,440
就像我们的身体是一个巨大的原子堆一样
just as our bodies are enormous piles of atoms.

59
00:03:50,550 --> 00:03:53,440
这些原子是怎样被排列在空间里
How those atoms are arranged in space

60
00:03:53,550 --> 00:03:55,810
取决于我们是谁，我们是什么
determines who and what we are.

61
00:03:55,920 --> 00:03:57,640
模型越大
The bigger the pattern,

62
00:03:57,760 --> 00:04:01,610
你就越需要到更遥远的地方找到副本
the further you have to travel before finding a repeat.

63
00:04:01,730 --> 00:04:03,880
当我说到原子重复的所有模型时
When l say all patterns of atoms repeat,

64
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:05,350
我真正的意思是所有的模型
l really mean all patterns,

65
00:04:05,460 --> 00:04:09,160
甚至构成我身体的原子的模型，Max Tegmark
even the pattern of atoms that makes up me, Max Tegmark.

66
00:04:09,270 --> 00:04:10,860
如果我走得足够远
lf l go far enough away,

67
00:04:10,970 --> 00:04:13,790
将又会有一个我的副本
there's gonna be another exact copy of me

68
00:04:13,910 --> 00:04:16,800
这个副本不仅看起来像我，而且感觉他就是我
who will not just look like me but feel like he is me.

69
00:04:16,910 --> 00:04:21,100
弗里曼：Max 计算他必须要走的光年数
Max has calculated the number of light-years he must travel

70
00:04:21,210 --> 00:04:25,710
来找到构成他身体的相同的原子排列
to find the same arrangement of atoms he is made of.

71
00:04:25,820 --> 00:04:29,340
这是一个令人吃惊的光年数
lt's a staggering number of light-years.

72
00:04:29,460 --> 00:04:34,460
Tegmark:我想出 1带有一百万个百万兆个百万兆个0
l came up with 1 with a million trillion trillion zeros

73
00:04:34,570 --> 00:04:35,570
跟在它后面
after it.

74
00:04:35,680 --> 00:04:37,550
这是一个巨大的数字
And that's a huge number.

75
00:04:37,660 --> 00:04:39,590
但是与我穷大比起来
But compared to infinity,

76
00:04:39,700 --> 00:04:42,990
我认为这是一个真实的空间尺寸
which is the true size of space, l think,

77
00:04:43,100 --> 00:04:45,890
它就在我们的后院中
it's just in our backyard.

78
00:04:46,010 --> 00:04:47,830
弗里曼：但是，这会让人迷惑
But there's a catch

79
00:04:47,940 --> 00:04:51,500
对于勇于长途旅行的探索者来说
for any explorer daring enough to brave the long trek

80
00:04:51,610 --> 00:04:53,270
怎样面对他的宇宙克隆体
to meet his cosmic clone.

81
00:04:53,380 --> 00:04:56,870
在20世纪20年代，天文学家哈勃
ln the 1920s, astronomer Edwin Hubble

82
00:04:56,980 --> 00:05:01,410
观察到我们的宇宙不是静止的
observed that our Universe was not sitting still.

83
00:05:01,520 --> 00:05:03,640
在他看到的每个方向上
ln every direction he looked,

84
00:05:03,760 --> 00:05:06,450
星系都在飞速远离我们
galaxies were flying away from us.

85
00:05:06,560 --> 00:05:07,850
看得越远
The further he looked,

86
00:05:07,960 --> 00:05:11,050
星系好像移动得越快
the faster these galaxies appeared to move.

87
00:05:11,160 --> 00:05:13,990
哈勃推断，只有一种可能性
Hubble concluded this was only possible

88
00:05:14,100 --> 00:05:16,820
就是假设整个宇宙在膨胀
if the entire Universe was expanding.

89
00:05:16,940 --> 00:05:19,730
Tegmark:如果这块混凝土块
lf this slab of concrete here

90
00:05:19,840 --> 00:05:22,530
是一个静止的遥远的星系
were a distant galaxy sitting still,

91
00:05:22,640 --> 00:05:24,430
那么我可以向它发送信息
then l could send messages to it.

92
00:05:24,540 --> 00:05:27,430
我可以以光速发送星系间的电子邮件
l could send intergalactic e-mails at the speed of light.

93
00:05:27,550 --> 00:05:31,340
如果我愿意等待足够长的时间，它们会到那里
lf l'm willing to wait long enough, they'll get there.

94
00:05:31,450 --> 00:05:32,610
既然空间正在扩展
Since space is expanding,

95
00:05:32,720 --> 00:05:34,470
我们需要打开传送带
we need to turn on the conveyer belt

96
00:05:34,590 --> 00:05:35,840
以便实现
to make this more realistic.

97
00:05:35,960 --> 00:05:41,110
这个遥远的星系实际上正在远离我
This distant galaxy is actually moving away from me.

98
00:05:41,230 --> 00:05:43,120
弗里曼：离我们越远
The further away from us we try to reach,

99
00:05:43,230 --> 00:05:47,420
空间膨胀得越快
the faster the space between here and there expands.

100
00:05:47,530 --> 00:05:49,360
必然在某一点上
At a certain point,

101
00:05:49,470 --> 00:05:52,360
以超光速扩张
the expansion is faster than the speed of light.

102
00:05:52,470 --> 00:05:56,230
这就意味着我们永远触及不到或者看不到
And that means we will never reach or even be able to see

103
00:05:56,340 --> 00:05:59,030
超过这个界限的任何事物
anything that lies beyond this horizon.

104
00:05:59,140 --> 00:06:01,530
对我来说，关于这一宇宙界限的坏消息
To me, the bad news with this cosmic horizon

105
00:06:01,650 --> 00:06:04,200
是它阻止我访问我自己和我朋友的副本
is it prevents me from visiting copies of myself

106
00:06:04,320 --> 00:06:07,110
这会是一件很滑稽的事
and of my friends, which would be kind of fun.

107
00:06:07,220 --> 00:06:11,240
弗里曼：但有寻找平行世界的另外一个地方——
But there's another place to look for parallel worlds --

108
00:06:11,360 --> 00:06:13,480
不是在外太空的深处
not in the depths of outer space,

109
00:06:13,590 --> 00:06:16,350
而是在我们的眼皮底下的微观世界
but in the microcosmos right under our noses,

110
00:06:16,460 --> 00:06:20,820
这里看上去没有什么是可靠的
where nothing, it seems, is solid.

111
00:06:20,930 --> 00:06:25,320
弗兰克·蒂普勒研究这种奇怪的量子力学规律
Frank Tipler studies the strange laws of quantum mechanics.

112
00:06:25,440 --> 00:06:29,000
这些规律统治着 构成我们身体的
These laws govern the tiny subatomic particles

113
00:06:29,110 --> 00:06:30,600
微小的亚原子粒子
we are all made of.

114
00:06:30,710 --> 00:06:32,270
谢谢你，Adam
Thank you, Adam.

115
00:06:32,380 --> 00:06:34,600
给他那惊人的大脑提供动力的
He fuels his prodigious brain power

116
00:06:34,710 --> 00:06:38,570
是新奥尔良所能提供的上等佳肴
with the finest cuisine the city of New Orleans has to offer.

117
00:06:38,680 --> 00:06:41,010
我想在今天这种情况下
l think what l would be in the mood for today

118
00:06:41,120 --> 00:06:42,750
是薛定谔法则
is the Schrodinger's Special.

119
00:06:48,090 --> 00:06:50,680
通过量子力学来描述所有的事物
All of reality is described by quantum mechanics,

120
00:06:50,800 --> 00:06:53,190
并且量子力学的核心方程式
and the central equation of quantum mechanics

121
00:06:53,300 --> 00:06:54,620
是薛定谔方程
is Schrodinger's Equation.

122
00:06:54,730 --> 00:06:58,860
弗里曼：欧文·薛定谔扫除了 我们所认知的事物的障碍
Erwin Schrodinger unplugged reality as we knew it

123
00:06:58,970 --> 00:07:01,990
当他写下同名方程式时
when he wrote down his namesake equation.

124
00:07:02,110 --> 00:07:05,600
他证明了亚原子粒子
He proved that subatomic particles could exist

125
00:07:05,710 --> 00:07:08,470
在同一时刻可能存在于多个地方
in multiple places at the same time

126
00:07:08,580 --> 00:07:10,800
而且不止一个粒子
and that more than one particle

127
00:07:10,920 --> 00:07:14,910
在同一时刻可能处于同一个点
could occupy the same point in space at the same time.

128
00:07:15,020 --> 00:07:19,350
这个亚原子的世界处于一个模糊的可能性
This subatomic world remains a blur of probabilities

129
00:07:19,460 --> 00:07:22,720
直到有人看到它的那一刻
until the moment someone looks at it,

130
00:07:22,830 --> 00:07:27,390
此时，这些可能性得到确定
when those probabilities gel into one definite reality.

131
00:07:27,500 --> 00:07:29,820
弗兰克相信这种量子不确定性
Frank believes this quantum fuzziness

132
00:07:29,940 --> 00:07:33,090
不仅仅发生在微观领域
doesn't just happen in the microscopic domain.

133
00:07:33,210 --> 00:07:36,660
它是万事万物的运行方式
lt's the way everything works.

134
00:07:36,780 --> 00:07:38,800
人们努力去想：“噢，不会是量子力学
People try to think, "Oh, it can't be

135
00:07:38,910 --> 00:07:40,730
适用于宇宙吧。”
quantum mechanic supplies to the Universe."

136
00:07:40,850 --> 00:07:41,870
毫无疑问
Nonsense.

137
00:07:41,980 --> 00:07:44,900
看到薛定谔方程的人
Anyone who looks at the Schrodinger Equation

138
00:07:45,020 --> 00:07:49,140
认定它适用于所有的事物
is forced to conclude that it applies to all of reality,

139
00:07:49,250 --> 00:07:52,750
不仅包括原子，而且包括房屋、玻璃
not just to atoms, but to buildings, to glass,

140
00:07:52,860 --> 00:07:54,510
以及整个宇宙
to the whole Universe.

141
00:07:54,630 --> 00:07:56,550
弗里曼：如果弗兰克是正确的
lf Frank is correct,

142
00:07:56,660 --> 00:07:59,120
那么每天的事物都是不确定的
everyday objects are schizophrenic

143
00:07:59,230 --> 00:08:00,990
在没人看它们时
when nobody looks at them.

144
00:08:01,100 --> 00:08:06,760
它们不断在各种存在中切换
They constantly shift between one of many existences.

145
00:08:06,870 --> 00:08:08,860
在这个金属罩的下面
Underneath this metal hood

146
00:08:08,970 --> 00:08:12,030
是弗兰克点的食物
is every dish that Frank could order,

147
00:08:12,140 --> 00:08:14,000
同时存在
existing simultaneously.

148
00:08:14,110 --> 00:08:16,500
而且只有当餐具被移走时
And only when the hood is removed

149
00:08:16,620 --> 00:08:19,970
弗兰克才看到事实
and Frank looks at it does one reality take hold.

150
00:08:20,090 --> 00:08:21,640
啊，牛肉
Ah. Veal.

151
00:08:21,750 --> 00:08:23,210
牡蛎
Oysters.

152
00:08:23,320 --> 00:08:26,350
但为什么盘中的食物不是别的呢？
But why this dish and not the other?

153
00:08:26,460 --> 00:08:29,420
大部分科学家认为弗兰克的观察
Most scientists believe Frank's observation

154
00:08:29,530 --> 00:08:32,490
自然而然地随机地做了一个决定
forced nature to make a decision at random.

155
00:08:32,600 --> 00:08:34,620
眨眼间
ln the blink of an eye,

156
00:08:34,730 --> 00:08:38,190
所有其他可能的食物不存在了
all other possible realities collapse.

157
00:08:38,300 --> 00:08:42,090
但弗兰克没有证明这一主流思想是正确的
But Frank is not convinced the mainstream has it right.

158
00:08:42,210 --> 00:08:45,870
他们认为当你使用一种测量方法时
They concluded that when you carry out a measurement,

159
00:08:45,980 --> 00:08:48,930
在它之外有潜在的宇宙
there are possible universes out there

160
00:08:49,050 --> 00:08:52,000
这个宇宙坍塌成真实的宇宙
which collapse into one real universe.

161
00:08:52,120 --> 00:08:54,310
实际上，这是反复无常的
Actually, that is inconsistent

162
00:08:54,420 --> 00:08:56,380
用量子力学观点
with the mathematics of quantum mechanics.

163
00:08:56,490 --> 00:09:00,350
弗里曼：弗兰克认为每个潜在的结果
Frank believes that every possible outcome

164
00:09:00,460 --> 00:09:02,550
实际上变成了现实
does in fact become reality,

165
00:09:02,660 --> 00:09:05,550
在它们自己的平行宇宙中
each in its own parallel universe.

166
00:09:05,660 --> 00:09:08,690
在这个宇宙中，弗兰克吃了牛肉
ln this Universe, Frank eats veal.

167
00:09:08,800 --> 00:09:12,530
但在另一个宇宙中，他吃了牛排
But in another universe, he ends up with steak.

168
00:09:12,640 --> 00:09:16,030
在别的另一个宇宙中，他吃了有虾的什锦菜肴
ln another, shrimp jambalaya.

169
00:09:16,140 --> 00:09:18,260
实际上，我们一直认为
ln fact, we have always known

170
00:09:18,380 --> 00:09:21,500
现实中有不止一个宇宙
that reality was more than one universe,

171
00:09:21,610 --> 00:09:25,600
但是物理学家不愿意相信他们自己的方程式
but physicists would not believe their own equations.

172
00:09:25,720 --> 00:09:27,610
弗里曼：如果弗兰克对的
lf Frank is right,

173
00:09:27,720 --> 00:09:31,910
你拥有的空间充满了一个几乎无边的海洋——
the space that you occupy is filled with a near infinite sea

174
00:09:32,020 --> 00:09:34,210
和你自己完全相同的副本的海洋
of identical copies of yourself.

175
00:09:34,330 --> 00:09:35,880
如果你身上发生了一件事
lf something happens to you,

176
00:09:35,990 --> 00:09:39,420
那么，你的其中一个隐藏的副本身上 则发生了恰好相反的事
the exact opposite could happen to one of your hidden twins.

177
00:09:39,530 --> 00:09:43,060
但是它们也许不会隐藏太长时间
But they may not stay hidden for long.

178
00:09:44,640 --> 00:09:47,830
这片硅芯片上有一个极小的目标
On this silicon chip lies a tiny object

179
00:09:47,940 --> 00:09:51,360
它可以同时存在两种真实
that can exist in two realities at once.

180
00:09:51,480 --> 00:09:53,570
这位科学家可能首次
For the first time,

181
00:09:53,680 --> 00:09:58,780
捕捉到了平行宇宙的迹象
this scientist may have caught sight of a parallel universe.

182
00:10:02,170 --> 00:10:05,700
交替的真实不需数光年远
Alternate realities don't have to be light-years away,

183
00:10:05,810 --> 00:10:07,760
遥远得穿过宇宙
far across the Universe.

184
00:10:07,880 --> 00:10:10,200
它们可能都在这里
They could all be right here.

185
00:10:10,770 --> 00:10:15,190
如果奇怪的量子力学法则是正确的
lf the strange rules of quantum mechanics are correct,

186
00:10:15,300 --> 00:10:19,260
我就是一个无数个其他我的集合
l am really a collection of countless alter egos,

187
00:10:19,380 --> 00:10:22,430
所有的其他我都纠结在不间断地斗争中
all locked in a relentless struggle.

188
00:10:22,550 --> 00:10:26,470
这场内部斗争一定是不可预料的
This internal war is supposed to be undetected.

189
00:10:26,580 --> 00:10:30,310
在任何时候，只有一个能够成为真正的我
At any moment, only one ego can become real.

190
00:10:30,420 --> 00:10:35,110
其他的是完全看不见的
The others are completely invisible.

191
00:10:35,220 --> 00:10:36,950
是这样吗？
Or are they?

192
00:10:39,230 --> 00:10:42,290
物理学家弗兰克·蒂普勒相信量子力学预示着
Physicist Frank Tipler believes quantum mechanics predicts

193
00:10:42,400 --> 00:10:45,320
我们自己有无数的副本
there are countless versions of ourselves

194
00:10:45,430 --> 00:10:47,390
生活在平行宇宙中
living in parallel universes.

195
00:10:47,500 --> 00:10:49,530
而且，像我们许多人一样
And like many of us,

196
00:10:49,640 --> 00:10:53,630
他有时幻想着转换自我
he sometimes daydreams of an alter ego.

197
00:10:53,740 --> 00:10:57,500
是时候围绕宇宙旅行了
lt's time for another trip around the cosmos

198
00:10:57,610 --> 00:10:59,200
跟随着勇敢的弗兰克
with fearless Frank,

199
00:10:59,320 --> 00:11:02,570
以及天体物理学家和宇宙探险家
astrophysicist and cosmic adventurenaut!

200
00:11:02,690 --> 00:11:04,240
本周
This week,

201
00:11:04,350 --> 00:11:11,150
弗兰克发现一个通向量子平行宇宙的突破口
Frank discovers a portal to a quantum parallel universe!

202
00:11:11,260 --> 00:11:16,120
弗里曼：弗兰克·蒂普勒相信 我们的宇宙就像动画片一样
This Frank Tipler believes our Universe, like a cartoon,

203
00:11:16,230 --> 00:11:19,620
诱导我们相信它是唯一真实的
tricks us into thinking it's the only reality.

204
00:11:19,740 --> 00:11:25,860
从前到后，动画片好像是一个唯一目标
From the top down, a cartoon appears to be a single object.

205
00:11:25,980 --> 00:11:29,800
但动画片由小单元组成
But a cartoon is made up of cells.

206
00:11:29,910 --> 00:11:36,610
我们可用一张透明幻灯片代表一个宇宙
We can use one transparency to represent one universe,

207
00:11:36,720 --> 00:11:41,980
但实际上它外面不只有四个宇宙
but in reality, there are four more universes out there,

208
00:11:42,090 --> 00:11:44,650
互相重叠
superimposed on each other.

209
00:11:44,760 --> 00:11:48,950
弗里曼：一个没人看见的物体 会同时存在于许多地方
An unobserved object will be in many places at once.

210
00:11:49,060 --> 00:11:50,420
根据弗兰克的说法
According to Frank,

211
00:11:50,530 --> 00:11:54,220
每个注定会成为现实
each one is destined to become reality

212
00:11:54,340 --> 00:11:58,060
在自己的平行宇宙中
in its own separate parallel universe.

213
00:11:58,170 --> 00:12:00,130
如果我们做个统计
When we make a measurement,

214
00:12:00,240 --> 00:12:03,600
每一个弗兰克·蒂普勒将会统一
each one of the Frank Tiplers would be united

215
00:12:03,710 --> 00:12:05,540
于一个特定结果
with a particular result.

216
00:12:05,650 --> 00:12:09,970
弗里曼：在一个宇宙中，目标只能在一个地方看见
ln one universe, the objects can only be seen in one place.

217
00:12:10,090 --> 00:12:11,370
从我们的视角中
From our perspective,

218
00:12:11,490 --> 00:12:13,680
观察行为好像
it appears the act of observation

219
00:12:13,790 --> 00:12:17,380
破坏了所有其他的可能
destroys all other possibilities.

220
00:12:17,490 --> 00:12:20,220
但这种现象可能是骗人的
But appearances can be deceptive.

221
00:12:20,330 --> 00:12:23,350
我们不应该把这个可能
We shouldn't think of the possibilities

222
00:12:23,470 --> 00:12:25,120
当成可能
just as possibilities.

223
00:12:25,230 --> 00:12:28,390
别的现实并没有终止而继续存在
The other realities do not cease to exist

224
00:12:28,500 --> 00:12:31,990
只是我选了一个在我手中
just because l have picked one in my own hand.

225
00:12:32,110 --> 00:12:35,730
它们仍然存在
They still remain. They still exist.

226
00:12:35,850 --> 00:12:39,470
弗里曼：如果我们能同时看见每个宇宙
lf we could see every universe at once,

227
00:12:39,580 --> 00:12:42,340
那么无数的弗兰克·蒂普勒
a countless number of Frank Tiplers

228
00:12:42,450 --> 00:12:45,740
会在同一时刻做同样的测量
will make the same measurement at the same time.

229
00:12:45,860 --> 00:12:48,310
而且每一个弗兰克都会
And each Frank will see the object

230
00:12:48,420 --> 00:12:50,410
在略有不同的地方看见这样的状况
in a slightly different place.

231
00:12:50,530 --> 00:12:54,450
这些极小的差别可能随着时间流逝被放大
These tiny nuances can, over time, amplify,

232
00:12:54,560 --> 00:12:59,020
导致平行世界的不同的命运
resulting in parallel worlds with diverging fates.

233
00:12:59,130 --> 00:13:00,920
在一个宇宙中
ln one universe,

234
00:13:01,040 --> 00:13:06,460
一个巨大的小行星错过地球，并且恐龙活了下来
a giant asteroid misses Earth and dinosaurs live on.

235
00:13:06,580 --> 00:13:14,070
在另一个宇宙中，火箭没有让美国输掉登月竞赛
ln another, rocket failure dooms America to lose the moon race.

236
00:13:14,180 --> 00:13:18,780
但我们怎么知道这些交替的现实是否存在
But how can we ever know if these alternate realities exist

237
00:13:18,890 --> 00:13:22,320
一旦我们看见它们，它们是否从视野中消失了？
if they evaporate from view as soon as we look at them?

238
00:13:24,890 --> 00:13:27,150
Cleland：我们从没有看见这些量子力学效应
We never see these quantum-mechanical effects,

239
00:13:27,260 --> 00:13:28,920
比如事物在同一时刻存在于两个地方
like being in two places at the same time,

240
00:13:29,030 --> 00:13:31,490
因为我们一直不可避免地测量事物
because we're always unavoidably measuring things.

241
00:13:31,600 --> 00:13:33,960
空气分子正撞击它们
Air molecules are hitting them.

242
00:13:34,070 --> 00:13:36,260
这是一种测量方法
That's a kind of measurement.

243
00:13:36,370 --> 00:13:37,790
我们向物体照射光线
We shine a light on something.

244
00:13:37,910 --> 00:13:39,260
这也是一种测量方法
That's a kind of measurement.

245
00:13:39,380 --> 00:13:41,400
也许有个物体位于桌子上
Perhaps you have an object sitting on a table.

246
00:13:41,510 --> 00:13:44,400
由于桌子是在支撑着目标
The table, because it's pushing back on the object,

247
00:13:44,510 --> 00:13:45,700
所以桌子也在测量目标
is also measuring it.

248
00:13:45,810 --> 00:13:48,470
弗里曼：实践物理学家 Andrew Cleland
Experimental physicist Andrew Cleland

249
00:13:48,580 --> 00:13:50,410
可能成为第一个
may have become the first man

250
00:13:50,520 --> 00:13:53,280
捕获到平行宇宙的一瞥的人
to catch a glimpse of a parallel universe.

251
00:13:53,390 --> 00:13:56,780
在这块小正方形硅中
lnside this small square of silicon,

252
00:13:56,890 --> 00:14:01,420
Andrew 刻蚀了一个能勉强看见的喷了金属的面
Andrew has etched a barely visible metal panel.

253
00:14:01,530 --> 00:14:05,320
由超过一万亿个原子组成
lt's made up of over a trillion atoms.

254
00:14:05,430 --> 00:14:08,690
在量子世界中，它是“哥斯拉”
ln the quantum world, it's Godzilla.

255
00:14:08,800 --> 00:14:13,030
并且他正在试着展示这块大的静止面
And he's trying to show that something this large

256
00:14:13,140 --> 00:14:16,130
遵循量子力学规律
still obeys the rules of quantum mechanics.

257
00:14:16,250 --> 00:14:18,300
我们很清楚这样的事实
We're very familiar with the fact

258
00:14:18,410 --> 00:14:21,200
量子力学适用于
that quantum mechanics applies to small things,

259
00:14:21,320 --> 00:14:22,710
像电子或原子一样的小的事物
like electrons or atoms.

260
00:14:22,820 --> 00:14:25,470
但是它应该也适用于
But the idea that it should also apply to large things,

261
00:14:25,590 --> 00:14:27,540
我们日常生活中面对的大的物体
things that we deal with in our everyday life,

262
00:14:27,660 --> 00:14:29,180
这样的想法很难被接受
is much harder to accept.

263
00:14:29,290 --> 00:14:32,720
弗里曼：只能证明这个刻蚀面遵循量子力学
But proving that his panel can behave quantum mechanically

264
00:14:32,830 --> 00:14:36,320
这意味着要保证没有东西测量这个面
means making sure nothing measures it.

265
00:14:36,430 --> 00:14:41,660
它需要被完全隔离以及极冷的条件
lt needs to be in complete isolation and very cold.

266
00:14:41,770 --> 00:14:44,790
Cleland：哦，这台仪器是一种稀释致冷机
So, this instrument is a dilution refrigerator.

267
00:14:44,910 --> 00:14:47,300
如果你有50万美元
lf you have half a million dollars lying around,

268
00:14:47,410 --> 00:14:49,470
也可以在自己家拥有一台
you, too, could own one in your own home.

269
00:14:49,580 --> 00:14:52,000
此仪器有一系列的温度范围
The instrument has a series of temperature stages

270
00:14:52,110 --> 00:14:56,970
从1开尔文到0.6开尔文再到0.1开尔文
that go from 1 Kelvin to .6 Kelvin to .1 Kelvin

271
00:14:57,090 --> 00:15:00,010
换句话说，所有的方式都到20毫开尔文
all the way to 20 Millikelvin, or in other words,

272
00:15:00,120 --> 00:15:03,710
大约零下459华氏度
about -459 degrees Fahrenheit.

273
00:15:03,830 --> 00:15:07,250
这就是我们做这些量子实验的地方
This is where we operate these quantum experiments.

274
00:15:07,360 --> 00:15:10,350
弗里曼：在稀释致冷机内
lnside the dilution refrigerator,

275
00:15:10,470 --> 00:15:14,290
Andrew把一个单一的能量子对准靶
Andrew directs a single quantum of energy at the paddle.

276
00:15:14,400 --> 00:15:16,990
应该有一半对一半的机会
lt should have a 50-50 chance

277
00:15:17,110 --> 00:15:21,300
要么收到能量要么失去能量
of either receiving the energy or missing it.

278
00:15:21,410 --> 00:15:23,830
然而，使他惊讶的是
lnstead, to his amazement,

279
00:15:23,950 --> 00:15:28,370
这个刻划面在同一时刻
the panel both receives the energy and misses it

280
00:15:28,480 --> 00:15:30,870
既收到能量又失去能量
at the same time

281
00:15:30,990 --> 00:15:35,750
并且开始在这两个现实之间周期性振荡
and begins to oscillate between these two realities.

282
00:15:35,860 --> 00:15:39,020
这个刻划面可能处于既无能量的状态
The panel can be both in a state where it has no energy

283
00:15:39,130 --> 00:15:42,120
又有能量的状态
and where it has one of these units of energy in it.

284
00:15:42,230 --> 00:15:43,820
实际上在这种状态下
And in that state, actually,

285
00:15:43,930 --> 00:15:46,260
同一时刻处于两种状态
when it's in both at the same time,

286
00:15:46,370 --> 00:15:50,060
你实际上确实得到一个每秒六十亿次的振荡
you actually do get a vibration at 6 billion times a second.

287
00:15:50,170 --> 00:15:53,500
弗里曼：Andrew 的突破性的实验
Andrew's groundbreaking experiment

288
00:15:53,610 --> 00:15:57,300
证明了奇怪的量子力学规律
proves that the strange laws of quantum mechanics

289
00:15:57,410 --> 00:16:00,140
支配我们宇宙的万事万物
govern everything in our Universe,

290
00:16:00,250 --> 00:16:03,010
从极小的粒子…
from tiny particles...

291
00:16:03,120 --> 00:16:05,510
到巨大的星系
to gargantuan galaxies.

292
00:16:05,620 --> 00:16:11,620
不论尺寸，任何物体可以同时存在于很多地方
No matter the size, any object can be in many places at once.

293
00:16:11,830 --> 00:16:14,450
Cleland：我认为物理学界的人们
l thought people within the physics community

294
00:16:14,560 --> 00:16:17,420
注意到这一点并且给它一些欢呼，因为它确实如此
would notice it and would give it some applause, as it were,

295
00:16:17,530 --> 00:16:19,590
但是我真地不认为它会打乱
but l really didn't expect that it would break

296
00:16:19,700 --> 00:16:22,330
我过去常常面对的学界之外
outside of the small community that l'm used to dealing with.

297
00:16:22,440 --> 00:16:23,660
那是十分令人惊奇的
So that was quite a surprise.

298
00:16:23,770 --> 00:16:25,730
有许多不同的解释
There's a number of different interpretations

299
00:16:25,840 --> 00:16:28,160
被提出来说明这一现象
that have been generated to try to explain this.

300
00:16:28,280 --> 00:16:31,130
其中一个就是多重世界
One of them is the many-worlds interpretation,

301
00:16:31,250 --> 00:16:32,570
或平行宇宙的解释
or parallel universes.

302
00:16:32,680 --> 00:16:35,440
但最终，那些是不可预测的
But in the end, those are not predictive.

303
00:16:35,550 --> 00:16:38,470
并且因为是不可预测的，所以是不能用实验验证的
And because it's not predictive, it's not something we can test.

304
00:16:38,590 --> 00:16:40,640
所以这让人们自由选择
So that leaves it open for people to choose

305
00:16:40,760 --> 00:16:42,550
他们喜欢的无论哪种解释
whichever interpretation they prefer.

306
00:16:42,660 --> 00:16:46,120
弗里曼：如果有量子平行宇宙
Freeman:lf there are quantum parallel universes,

307
00:16:46,230 --> 00:16:49,320
Andrew的实验可能是迈向
Andrew's experiment could be the first step

308
00:16:49,430 --> 00:16:51,590
解开秘密的第一步
toward unlocking their secrets.

309
00:16:53,600 --> 00:16:56,760
但是有别的类型的平行世界
But there's another type of parallel world

310
00:16:56,870 --> 00:17:00,760
几乎所有的科学家相信应该存在
that nearly all scientists are convinced should exist.

311
00:17:00,880 --> 00:17:02,570
它由反物质组成
lt's made of antimatter,

312
00:17:02,680 --> 00:17:04,300
并且如果我们遇到它
and if we ever meet it,

313
00:17:04,410 --> 00:17:09,250
结果将是完全彻底地毁灭
the results would be complete and utter destruction.

314
00:17:11,430 --> 00:17:15,190
在宇宙中潜藏有邪恶的力量
There's something evil lurking out in the cosmos.

315
00:17:15,640 --> 00:17:18,590
叫做反物质
lt's called antimatter.

316
00:17:19,100 --> 00:17:23,130
如果一小滴反物质滴到正常物质上
lf one tiny drop of it met a drop of normal matter,

317
00:17:23,240 --> 00:17:27,700
它会爆发出比广岛原子弹还强大的力量
it would explode with more force than the bomb at Hiroshima.

318
00:17:31,710 --> 00:17:33,370
令人恐惧的现实
The frightening reality

319
00:17:33,480 --> 00:17:37,910
是宇宙大爆炸应该产的反物质和物质
is that the Big Bang should have created antimatter and matter

320
00:17:38,020 --> 00:17:39,310
应该是等量的
in equal amounts.

321
00:17:39,420 --> 00:17:41,910
换句话说，在别处
ln other words, out there,

322
00:17:42,020 --> 00:17:46,880
应该有一个反物质孪生宇宙——相对于我们的宇宙
there should be an antimatter twin to our Universe

323
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:51,220
具有毁灭所有物体的力量
with the power to annihilate all creation.

324
00:17:51,330 --> 00:17:53,760
只是有一个问题
There's just one problem.

325
00:17:53,870 --> 00:17:59,600
这个致命的反物质宇宙下落不明
This deadly antimatter universe has gone missing.

326
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:03,290
Joanne Hewett是一位理论物理学家
Joanne Hewett is a theoretical physicist

327
00:18:03,400 --> 00:18:07,330
工作于加利福尼亚的斯坦福直线性加速器中心
at the Stanford Linear Accelerator in California.

328
00:18:07,440 --> 00:18:08,800
她是位侦测专家
She's a detective

329
00:18:08,910 --> 00:18:12,570
跟踪下落不明的平行宇宙
on the trail of this missing parallel universe.

330
00:18:12,680 --> 00:18:13,870
它到哪去了呢？
Where did it go?

331
00:18:13,980 --> 00:18:17,440
那就是说，当今科学的一大秘密
That's one of the big mysteries of science today,

332
00:18:17,550 --> 00:18:19,640
是反物质去了哪里？
is where did the antimatter go?

333
00:18:19,750 --> 00:18:25,440
每个粒子都有它自己的反粒子
Every particle has its own antiparticle associated with it.

334
00:18:25,560 --> 00:18:29,980
反粒子和它的同源粒子有着同样的质量
lt has the same mass as its parent particle,

335
00:18:30,100 --> 00:18:31,850
但它具有全然相反的特性
but it has all the opposite properties.

336
00:18:31,960 --> 00:18:37,090
像电子有一个与之关联的电荷一样
Like an electron has an electric charge associated with it,

337
00:18:37,200 --> 00:18:39,930
因此一个被称作正电子的反电子
so an antielectron, which is called a positron,

338
00:18:40,040 --> 00:18:44,100
有相反的电荷与之关联
has the opposite electric charge associated with it.

339
00:18:44,210 --> 00:18:46,170
当反物质和物质撞到一起时
When antimatter and matter collide,

340
00:18:46,280 --> 00:18:48,070
总的来说会得到一个能量大爆炸
basically you get a big burst of energy,

341
00:18:48,180 --> 00:18:49,500
就像“嘣”的一声
just like "Boom."

342
00:18:49,620 --> 00:18:54,710
弗里曼：如果“大爆炸”创造的反物质和物质一样多
lf the Big Bang created as much antimatter as matter,

343
00:18:54,820 --> 00:18:59,010
那么，应该发了两件事情中的一件
then one of two things should have happened to it.

344
00:18:59,120 --> 00:19:01,850
任何反物质和物质的粒子
Any particles of antimatter and matter

345
00:19:01,960 --> 00:19:03,750
相互碰撞
that bump into one another

346
00:19:03,860 --> 00:19:06,720
应该完全湮灭
should have been completely annihilated.

347
00:19:06,830 --> 00:19:11,060
但一些反物质和物质可能仍然没有碰到过
But some antimatter and matter may not yet have met,

348
00:19:11,170 --> 00:19:15,190
因此一次剧烈的碰撞可能正朝我们走来
and an explosive reunion could be coming our way.

349
00:19:15,310 --> 00:19:18,560
Joanne 以找出其真相作为她的职业
Joanne has made it her business to find out.

350
00:19:18,680 --> 00:19:21,500
她掌管着斯坦福的
She harnessed the power of Stanford's

351
00:19:21,610 --> 00:19:23,840
两英里长的粒子加速器的动力
2-mile long particle accelerator

352
00:19:23,950 --> 00:19:28,670
以此重新创造缩小版的“宇宙大爆炸”
to re-create miniature versions of the Big Bang.

353
00:19:28,790 --> 00:19:31,340
我们创造了两个粒子束
We created two beams of particles,

354
00:19:31,460 --> 00:19:34,650
并且用恰好足够的能量使它们碰撞
and we collided them with just enough energy

355
00:19:34,760 --> 00:19:38,080
借此产生一对叫做B介子的粒子
to create a pair of particles called B mesons.

356
00:19:38,200 --> 00:19:43,120
弗里曼：在她的实验中，叫做“B”因子
lnside their experiment, called the "B" factoring,

357
00:19:43,240 --> 00:19:45,630
Joanne 及一个科学团队
Joanne and a team of scientists

358
00:19:45,740 --> 00:19:50,330
跟踪B介子和被称为
track the swarm of B mesons and their antimatter twins,

359
00:19:50,440 --> 00:19:51,930
反B介子的反物质群
known as Anti-Bs.

360
00:19:52,040 --> 00:19:54,730
寻找任何细微的差异
They looked for any subtle differences

361
00:19:54,850 --> 00:19:58,470
在这些据推测等价相反的粒子之中
between these supposedly equal and opposite particles.

362
00:19:58,580 --> 00:20:02,170
如果你想研究B介子和反B介子
lf you want to study the B meson versus the Anti-B meson,

363
00:20:02,290 --> 00:20:05,680
你就必须能看见不同
you have to be able to see the difference in the time

364
00:20:05,790 --> 00:20:08,210
在两者消失的时刻
between which the two of them decay.

365
00:20:08,330 --> 00:20:10,920
但它们存在一万亿分之一秒
But they live for a trillionth of a second.

366
00:20:11,030 --> 00:20:12,920
这使得它们很难被研究
lt makes them very difficult to study.

367
00:20:13,030 --> 00:20:16,590
弗里曼：在精密仪器的帮助下
With the help of sophisticated instruments,

368
00:20:16,700 --> 00:20:20,030
Joanne 和她的团队发现了一个极小的不同
Joanne and her team discovered a tiny difference

369
00:20:20,140 --> 00:20:22,090
在B介子和反B介子之间
between the B and the Anti-B.

370
00:20:22,210 --> 00:20:24,600
一种会比另一种消失得仅仅快一点儿
One will decay just a little bit faster

371
00:20:24,710 --> 00:20:27,500
或者说两者消失的时刻不同
or a little bit differently than the other one.

372
00:20:27,610 --> 00:20:31,240
并且正是反B介子衰减得快一点儿
And it's the Anti-B meson that decays just a little bit faster.

373
00:20:31,350 --> 00:20:34,970
弗里曼：物质和反物质之间的这个极小的不平衡
This tiny imbalance between matter and antimatter

374
00:20:35,090 --> 00:20:36,740
可能产生一个大差异
could make a big difference

375
00:20:36,860 --> 00:20:39,250
对宇宙大爆炸之后发生的情况
to what happened after the Big Bang.

376
00:20:39,360 --> 00:20:44,150
实际上，这可以产生一个全然不同的宇宙
ln fact, it could make a whole universe of difference.

377
00:20:44,260 --> 00:20:47,550
想想物质和反物质
Think of matter and antimatter

378
00:20:47,670 --> 00:20:51,100
被放在国际跳棋游戏中…
locked in a game of checkers...

379
00:20:52,940 --> 00:20:56,630
…直到死亡
...to the death.

380
00:20:56,740 --> 00:20:59,600
双方以相等的数量开始
Both sides start with equal numbers.

381
00:20:59,710 --> 00:21:01,500
随着时间流逝
As time goes on,

382
00:21:01,610 --> 00:21:04,200
大部分的跳棋子和反跳棋子
most of the checkers and anti-checkers

383
00:21:04,320 --> 00:21:05,870
互相歼灭
annihilate one another.

384
00:21:06,920 --> 00:21:12,850
但最终，一方有一点儿优势
But in the end, one side has a slight edge

385
00:21:12,960 --> 00:21:15,680
并宣告获胜
and claims victory.

386
00:21:15,790 --> 00:21:19,150
来自整个组的这一颗跳棋子
This one single checker out of that entire group

387
00:21:19,260 --> 00:21:23,360
是由宇宙大爆炸创造的，是今天存在着的一切
that was created from the Big Bang is all that exists today,

388
00:21:23,470 --> 00:21:26,790
并且是宇宙的全部物质含量
and it is the entire matter content of the Universe.

389
00:21:26,800 --> 00:21:31,470
弗里曼：今天我们在宇宙中看见的物质 仅仅是极小的一部分
The matter we see in the Universe today is just a tiny fraction

390
00:21:31,480 --> 00:21:35,270
在物质和反物质这两个巨大的海洋中
of two vast seas of matter and antimatter.

391
00:21:35,380 --> 00:21:37,940
它们会彻底地互相毁灭
They would have completely destroyed one another

392
00:21:38,050 --> 00:21:41,070
要不是因为有极小的不平衡
were it not for the tiny imbalance

393
00:21:41,190 --> 00:21:46,380
Joanne和她的同事发现的B和反B介子之间的 不平衡
Joanne and her colleagues found between the B and Anti-B mesons.

394
00:21:46,490 --> 00:21:48,010
Hewitt：由于反物质
Because of this slight difference

395
00:21:48,130 --> 00:21:50,450
在衰减特性方面的极小的差异
in the decay properties of the antimatter,

396
00:21:50,560 --> 00:21:53,420
我们今天留下了物质，仅仅一点儿的物质
we're left with matter today, just a sliver of matter

397
00:21:53,530 --> 00:21:56,160
与宇宙大爆炸的最初数量相比
compared to the original amount of the Big Bang.

398
00:21:59,770 --> 00:22:02,190
弗里曼：那么，Joanne所做的探测工作是合乎逻辑的
So, Joanne's detective work is done,

399
00:22:02,310 --> 00:22:04,200
每个人可以不必担忧
and everyone can stop worrying

400
00:22:04,310 --> 00:22:06,300
我们即将毁灭
about our impending annihilation

401
00:22:06,410 --> 00:22:08,430
邪恶的反物质宇宙
with an evil antimatter universe

402
00:22:08,550 --> 00:22:11,170
徘徊在空间的黑暗隐蔽处
prowling in the dark recesses of space.

403
00:22:11,280 --> 00:22:14,410
实际上，这个故事可能不是很圆满
Actually, this story may not wrap up so neatly.

404
00:22:14,520 --> 00:22:17,540
因为尽管“B”因子这一结论
Because even though the "B"-factoring results

405
00:22:17,660 --> 00:22:20,650
解释了大部分反物质去了哪里…
explain where most of the antimatter went...

406
00:22:20,760 --> 00:22:23,420
遗憾地是，那没有解释清
Unfortunately, that doesn't account

407
00:22:23,530 --> 00:22:27,120
宇宙中所有下落不明的反物质
for all of the missing antimatter in the Universe.

408
00:22:27,230 --> 00:22:30,920
因此，必然有一些新的物理现象
So there must be some new piece of physics

409
00:22:31,040 --> 00:22:34,530
仍处在那里等待我们探索
that is still there for us to discover.

410
00:22:34,640 --> 00:22:38,500
3,2,1,0，启动
3, 2, 1, zero, and liftoff

411
00:22:38,610 --> 00:22:42,770
最终发射了“奋进号”航天飞机
for the final launch of Endeavour.

412
00:22:42,880 --> 00:22:46,770
弗里曼：那个探索可能近在身边
That discovery could be close at hand.

413
00:22:46,890 --> 00:22:50,140
在2011年5月，奋进号航天飞机
ln May 2011, the space shuttle Endeavour

414
00:22:50,260 --> 00:22:54,180
把一台叫做AMS（高级映射光谱测量仪）的 巨大的粒子探测器传送到了
delivered a giant particle detector called the AMS

415
00:22:54,290 --> 00:22:56,450
国际空间站
to the lnternational Space Station.

416
00:22:56,560 --> 00:22:58,820
这台AMS将寻找宇宙射线
The AMS will look for the cosmic rays

417
00:22:58,930 --> 00:23:02,490
由数十亿年前宇宙大爆炸之后
created billions of years ago from matter and antimatter

418
00:23:02,600 --> 00:23:06,790
彻底毁灭的物质和反物质所产生的
annihilating in the wake of the Big Bang.

419
00:23:06,910 --> 00:23:10,130
可能宇宙射线会引导我们找到
Perhaps they will lead us to the remnants

420
00:23:10,240 --> 00:23:13,200
反物质的原始的海洋的残余部分
of that primordial sea of antimatter,

421
00:23:13,310 --> 00:23:16,600
如果这个残余部分脱离了那里
if it's out there.

422
00:23:16,720 --> 00:23:20,410
反物质宇宙可能潜行于我们的宇宙的边缘
The antimatter universe is not the only strange domain

423
00:23:20,520 --> 00:23:23,910
它不是唯一的陌生的领域
that may be lurking on the fringes of our cosmos.

424
00:23:24,020 --> 00:23:28,310
我们可能被各种各样的平行宇宙包围
We could be surrounded by all manner of parallel universes.

425
00:23:28,430 --> 00:23:33,050
了解平行宇宙在哪里，它们是什么不仅仅是一个空想的游戏
Learning where and what they are is not just a theoretical game.

426
00:23:33,170 --> 00:23:36,290
整个宇宙的命运就靠它了
The fate of our entire Universe depends on it.

427
00:23:38,320 --> 00:23:43,080
我们为什么关心是否有其他版本的你和我存在？
Why do we care if another version of you and me exists?

428
00:23:43,190 --> 00:23:47,250
我们不想面对面地遇到他们
We're never going to meet them face-to-face.

429
00:23:47,360 --> 00:23:50,720
但这些另一个自我是重要的
But these alter egos are important.

430
00:23:50,840 --> 00:23:54,360
因为他们是回答关于生存的
Because they are the key to answering

431
00:23:54,470 --> 00:23:58,000
最大问题的关键——
the biggest questions of existence --

432
00:23:58,110 --> 00:24:01,930
宇宙是怎样诞生的…
how was the Universe born...

433
00:24:02,050 --> 00:24:04,950
以及会怎样结束？
and how will it end?

434
00:24:09,500 --> 00:24:12,150
宇宙学家Andrei Linde相信
Cosmologist Andrei Linde believes

435
00:24:12,270 --> 00:24:14,320
他做了一项令人震惊的探索
he's made a shocking discovery.

436
00:24:14,440 --> 00:24:17,090
创造宇宙的力量
The force that created the cosmos

437
00:24:17,210 --> 00:24:21,330
应该创造了无数的其他平行宇宙
should have created countless other parallel universes.

438
00:24:21,440 --> 00:24:22,800
而且同样的力量
And that same force

439
00:24:22,910 --> 00:24:26,440
也会导致我们所知的每一种事物死亡
would also cause the demise of everything we know.

440
00:24:26,550 --> 00:24:30,310
这项探索开始于一个简单的
This discovery began with a simple,

441
00:24:30,420 --> 00:24:33,310
表面上看来平凡的问题
seemingly innocuous question.

442
00:24:33,420 --> 00:24:36,080
为什么宇宙在我们看到的每个方向上
Why does the Universe look the same

443
00:24:36,190 --> 00:24:38,410
看起来一样？
in every direction that we look?

444
00:24:38,530 --> 00:24:41,820
这个问题困扰物理学家们
This is the problem which bothered physicists

445
00:24:41,930 --> 00:24:43,950
几百年了
for, well, hundreds of years.

446
00:24:44,070 --> 00:24:48,860
弗里曼：Andrei深信 仅仅一个大规模的混乱的爆炸
Andrei was convinced that a massive chaotic explosion alone

447
00:24:48,970 --> 00:24:52,460
不会创造出我们今天看到的
could never create the smooth and uniform Universe

448
00:24:52,570 --> 00:24:53,830
光滑的均匀的宇宙
that we see today.

449
00:24:53,940 --> 00:24:57,370
因为用物理方法了解宇宙大爆炸
Because the way physics understands the Big Bang,

450
00:24:57,480 --> 00:24:59,700
所以我们今天看到的每件事物
everything we see today

451
00:24:59,820 --> 00:25:04,510
从一块方糖大小的东西爆发出来
exploded from something the size of a sugar cube.

452
00:25:04,620 --> 00:25:07,540
Linde：好像为了创造
lt appears that in order to create

453
00:25:07,660 --> 00:25:10,280
我们周围的所有星系
all of these galaxies surrounding us

454
00:25:10,390 --> 00:25:12,520
在公认的宇宙大爆炸理论中
in the standard Big Bang theory,

455
00:25:12,630 --> 00:25:16,020
我们大约需要10的85次方吨的
we would need appro ximately 10 to the degree 85

456
00:25:16,130 --> 00:25:18,220
高能炸药
tons of high-energy explosive.

457
00:25:20,400 --> 00:25:22,790
这看上去是十亿个十亿个十亿个十亿个
lt's like a billion of a billion of a billion of billion

458
00:25:22,910 --> 00:25:25,090
十亿个十亿个十亿个十亿吨的
of a billion of a billion of a billion of a billion

459
00:25:25,210 --> 00:25:26,900
物质
tons of matter.

460
00:25:27,010 --> 00:25:30,000
这听起来的确完全令人难以置信
And this just sounds totally unbelievable.

461
00:25:30,110 --> 00:25:32,200
所以有必要
And that's why it was necessary

462
00:25:32,310 --> 00:25:35,100
从另外一个故事说起
to come with a different kind of story.

463
00:25:35,220 --> 00:25:37,950
它就是膨胀理论
And that was the inflationary theory.

464
00:25:40,160 --> 00:25:43,380
弗里曼：膨胀是一个Andrei很清楚的过程
lnflation is a process Andrei knows very well,

465
00:25:43,490 --> 00:25:46,480
它不仅仅作为扩大宇宙的动力
and not just as a force that blows up the Universe.

466
00:25:46,600 --> 00:25:50,050
当Andrei在不分析宇宙大爆炸的秘密时
When he's not decoding the mysteries of the Big Bang,

467
00:25:50,170 --> 00:25:54,690
他用他喜欢的业务爱好 扩大了我们的世界的一小部分 -
he inflates tiny portions of our world with his favorite hobby --

468
00:25:54,800 --> 00:25:56,700
摄影
photography.

469
00:25:58,370 --> 00:26:01,400
Linde：这真是一台非常不错的相机
This is actually a pretty nice camera.

470
00:26:01,510 --> 00:26:05,430
在我60岁时，我的家人把它作为生日礼物送给我
My family gave it to me as a birthday present when l got 60,

471
00:26:05,550 --> 00:26:08,450
并且从那个时候起，我获得了拍照的方法
and since that time, l got kind of hooked.

472
00:26:09,820 --> 00:26:11,540
弗里曼：根据Andrei的理论
According to Andrei's theory,

473
00:26:11,650 --> 00:26:14,680
当宇宙处于这块方糖的大小时
when the Universe was the size of this sugar cube,

474
00:26:14,790 --> 00:26:17,880
它一定是一个混乱的能量团
it must have been a chaotic lump of energy,

475
00:26:17,990 --> 00:26:19,680
每个微小的部分
each microscopic part

476
00:26:19,800 --> 00:26:23,450
都完全不同
a fundamentally different place from another,

477
00:26:23,570 --> 00:26:26,720
像一个彩色的混合物
like a technicolor patchwork.

478
00:26:26,840 --> 00:26:30,690
Linde：这个立方体可能有不同大小的各种颜色
This cube may have different colors of different size,

479
00:26:30,810 --> 00:26:32,260
但膨胀干了什么呢
but what inflation does,

480
00:26:32,370 --> 00:26:35,560
以方糖的任意角落作为开始
if you start with any tiny corner of the sugar cube,

481
00:26:35,680 --> 00:26:38,130
那么，这个部分的宇宙扩大了
then this part of the Universe expands.

482
00:26:38,250 --> 00:26:39,700
而且我们环顾周围
And we look around.

483
00:26:39,820 --> 00:26:42,610
我们正居住在这块方糖的里面
We're living inside this sugar cube.

484
00:26:42,720 --> 00:26:46,110
我们周围的每一件事物都是白的或者都是粉红的
Everything is white around us or everything is pink around us.

485
00:26:46,220 --> 00:26:48,310
这样我们就说，“噢，太美了。”
And then we say, "Oh, so beautiful."

486
00:26:48,420 --> 00:26:51,520
我们的宇宙是均匀的 我们解答了所有的问题
Our Universe is uniform. We've solved all our problems.

487
00:26:53,200 --> 00:26:56,790
弗里曼：但膨胀可能不会解决所有的问题
But inflation may not solve all of our problems.

488
00:26:56,900 --> 00:26:59,420
因为一旦膨胀开始
Because once it gets going, inflation,

489
00:26:59,530 --> 00:27:02,560
像Andrei痴迷摄影一样
like Andrei's addiction to blow-up photography,

490
00:27:02,670 --> 00:27:04,160
它就永不停息
never stops.

491
00:27:04,270 --> 00:27:07,600
看起来宇宙
The Universe, it seems,

492
00:27:07,710 --> 00:27:12,670
正持续不断地变得越来越大
is constantly growing larger and larger forever.

493
00:27:12,780 --> 00:27:14,570
但根据Andrei的观点
But according to Andrei,

494
00:27:14,680 --> 00:27:16,810
膨胀也必然要返回来
inflation must also come back

495
00:27:16,920 --> 00:27:19,740
拜访我们已经扩张了的宇宙
to visit our already expanded Universe.

496
00:27:19,860 --> 00:27:23,750
它瞄向宇宙的一个极小部分
lt zeros in on a tiny fraction of it

497
00:27:23,860 --> 00:27:27,780
并且把它放大成为一个新宇宙
and blows it up into a new universe.

498
00:27:27,900 --> 00:27:30,020
Linde：所以，会发生什么——
So, what will happen --

499
00:27:30,130 --> 00:27:33,860
会有一个新形成的泡泡
there will be a bubble formed of a new phase, if you wish,

500
00:27:33,970 --> 00:27:36,790
并且这个泡泡会抓住并杀死你
and the bubble will grab you and kill you,

501
00:27:36,910 --> 00:27:38,930
并且那会是终结
and, well, that will be the end.

502
00:27:39,040 --> 00:27:41,500
弗里曼：如果Andrei是正确的
lf Andrei is correct,

503
00:27:41,610 --> 00:27:44,800
那么，我们的宇宙是不稳定的
our Universe is inherently unstable.

504
00:27:44,910 --> 00:27:50,040
随时，它的一小部分扩展到极大的宇宙尺寸
At any moment, one tiny part of it might expand to cosmic size

505
00:27:50,150 --> 00:27:53,810
并且破坏我们所知的一切事物
and destroy everything we know.

506
00:27:53,920 --> 00:27:55,780
但在美洲大陆对面
But across the continent,

507
00:27:55,890 --> 00:27:59,620
一位以前的同事认为Andrei误解了膨胀理论
a former colleague thinks Andrei has got it wrong.

508
00:27:59,730 --> 00:28:04,090
Paul Steinhardt是科学界的“阿尔伯特·爱因斯坦教授”
Paul Steinhardt is the Albert Einstein professor in science

509
00:28:04,200 --> 00:28:05,990
任职于普林斯顿大学
at Princeton University,

